course: books
lesson: 86
title: Just So Stories, How the First Letter Was Written, part 3: «a great invention» and the road on Merrow Down
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: «Explain! Explain! Explain!» shouts the whole Tribe. Tegumai says there were no bad people at all, only the poor Stranger-man they are trying to choke. Then Taffy owns up, not feeling very comfy, that she gave him the picture, and her Daddy puts his arm round her.
explain: Taffy explains: there was only one spear, drawn three times to make sure; the «bad people» were her beavers; Mummy had her hands up because she would be pleased. Then she tells the whole Tribe they are «just the stupidest people in the world» and orders them to wash the nice Stranger-man.
explain: After a long silence the Head Chief laughs, then the Stranger-man, then Tegumai, then everyone, except the ladies, who keep saying «Idiot!» to their husbands. The Head Chief says Taffy has made a great invention which men will one day call writing, though pictures are not always understood.
explain: Taffy only wanted the spear, and in the end it is the one spear nobody brought. The Stranger-man is adopted into the Tribe because he was a gentleman about the mud.
explain: In the poem, Kipling places the story on Merrow Down near Guildford in Surrey, England, where the river Wey, which Taffy called Wagai, runs. Long before ancient Britons traded there, he says, Taffy and her Daddy had their home on that down.
fact: Tegumai to his Tribe: «Can't a man break his carp-spear without the whole countryside descending on him? You're a very interfering people.»
fact: Taffy: «There wasn't lots of spears. There was only one spear. I drawded it three times to make sure.» «Drawded» is her baby way of saying drew.
fact: Taffy explains the spear looked as if it «stuck into Daddy's head» because «there wasn't room on the birch-bark», and that the «bad people» were her beavers showing the way through the swamp.
fact: The laughing goes in order: the Head Chief, then the Stranger-man, then Tegumai «till he fell down flat on the bank», then all the Tribe; Teshumai and the ladies did not laugh.
fact: The Head Chief: «It is a great invention, and some day men will call it writing. At present it is only pictures, and ... pictures are not always properly understood.»
fact: He foretells «letters, all twenty-six of 'em», when «we shall be able to read as well as to write».
fact: His advice: next time «send a man who can talk our language with it, to explain what it means».
fact: The Stranger-man is adopted into the Tribe of Tegumai «because he was a gentleman and did not make a fuss about the mud».
fact: Kipling's last joke, that little girls do not like learning to read and write, is an old idea that is wrong: girls and boys learn to read and write equally well, and many great writers are women.
fact: The poem names real places in Surrey: Merrow Down, Guildford, the river Wey, Broadstone brook, Bramley, Shere and Shamley; it says the Wey «Was more than six times bigger then».
fact: Real life: the twenty-six letters of the English alphabet are true, but they came thousands of years after the Stone Age; the Head Chief could not really have known about them.
fact: Real life: the first true writing grew from pictures and counting marks used by traders and temple workers in Mesopotamia about 5,000 years ago, to keep records of grain, sheep and beer.
fact: Real life: Stone Age people in Britain did not write; writing came to Britain with the Romans, about 2,000 years ago.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «I'm dead» = that's so funny; «eejit» (Ireland) = idiot; «howay» (North East England) = come on; «salty» = cross, bitter.
exercise: What does the whole Tribe shout at Tegumai? | «Explain! Explain! Explain!» | «Off with his head, and the Stranger's too!» | «Where is the spear?» | «Humph!»
exercise: What does Tegumai call his Tribe? | A very interfering people | A kind and quiet tribe | The cleverest people in the world | Silly beavers
exercise: Teshumai asks where the bad people are. What does Tegumai say? | There weren't any; his only visitor was the Stranger-man | They ran off into the woods when they heard the drums | They are hiding in the beaver-swamp, waiting for dark | They were the beavers
exercise: How does Taffy feel when she owns up about the picture? | Not quite comfy | Very proud of herself | Frightened to tears | Bored with it all
exercise: What does her Daddy do when the Tribe cries «You?» | Puts his arm round her, so she doesn't care | Hides her behind his back and tells the Tribe she is not there | Tells her off | Runs away with her
exercise: How many spears were there really in the story? | One, drawn three times | Three different spears, one for each of the Tribe's chiefs | Two | Twenty-six
exercise: Why did the spear look as if it stuck into Daddy's head? | There wasn't room on the birch-bark | Taffy wanted it to look exciting, like a real battle story | Daddy really was hurt | The Stranger-man scratched it
exercise: What does Taffy say the «bad people» were? | Her beavers, drawn to show the way through the swamp | The Neolithic ladies, all at lunch together, drawn in a row of six | Some trees | Her own family
exercise: What does Taffy call the whole Tribe? | Just the stupidest people in the world | The bravest tribe in all the land | Very kind | A noisy crowd of fishermen who frighten the carp away
exercise: What does Taffy order them to do with the Stranger-man? | Wash him | Feed him a big lunch | Send him home | Give him a spear
exercise: Who laughs first? | The Head Chief | The Stranger-man | Tegumai, her Daddy | Little Taffy herself
exercise: Who does NOT laugh? | Teshumai and the Neolithic ladies | The Head Chief and all his Hetmans, Platoffs and Dolmans | Tegumai | The Stranger-man
exercise: What do the ladies keep saying to their husbands? | «Idiot!» | «Explain!» | «Most shocking!» | «Well done!»
exercise: What does the Head Chief say men will one day call Taffy's invention? | Writing | Picture-drawing | Painting | Scratching
exercise: How many letters does the Head Chief say there will be? | Twenty-six | Twenty-two | Six | A hundred
exercise: Which spear did the Tribe forget to bring? | Daddy's black-handled spear | Every spear the Tribe owned | The Head Chief's spear | The broken spear
exercise: What advice does the Head Chief give for the next picture-letter? | Send a man who can talk our language with it, to explain | Draw it larger on a bigger piece of bark | Never draw beavers or hills in a letter again | Use colours
exercise: Why is the Stranger-man adopted into the Tribe? | He was a gentleman and made no fuss about the mud | He was the best fisherman on the river | Taffy begged them to, and nobody dared say no to her | He brought the spear
exercise: Where does the poem place Taffy's home? | On Merrow Down, near Guildford in Surrey | By the Amazon in Brazil | On the Limpopo | In a cave near London Bridge, by the river Thames
exercise: What did Taffy call the river Wey? | Wagai | Wey-ya | Limpopo | Merrow
exercise: Looking back: in part 2, what did the ladies do to the Stranger-man? | Sat on him in a line of six and filled his hair with mud | Gave him lunch | Asked him many polite questions | Tied him up with reindeer-sinews and left him by the fire
exercise: Looking back: what was Tegumai's spear made of? | Wood with shark's teeth at the end | Stone, chipped sharp | Iron from a far tribe, hammered to a point | Bone
test: Ruby: «Head Chief was salty and punished Taffy, end of.» (salty = cross) Put it right. | He laughed and said she had made a great invention | Right, she was spanked by the whole Tribe, one chief after another, all afternoon | He sent her away from the Tribe | He made her wash the Stranger-man herself
test: Why does the Head Chief say pictures need «a man who can talk our language» with them? | A picture alone can be misunderstood, as they have just seen | The Stranger-man cannot see pictures | Pictures are against the old law of the Tribe of Tegumai | Only men may carry them
test: What would have happened if Taffy had drawn the spear once, small and clear, beside Mummy? | Perhaps less fright, but a picture could still be misread | The Tribe would certainly not have come, and everyone would have understood at once | Teshumai would have written back | Nothing could ever be misread
test: Which is TRUE at the end of this story? | Taffy's Daddy's black-handled spear is never brought | The Stranger-man goes home angry, with mud still in his hair | The Tribe punishes Taffy | Tegumai catches a huge carp
test: Kipling ends: very few little girls like learning to read or write. What should we say about that today? | It is an old idea that is wrong: girls read and write as well as boys | It is true even today, which is why most writers in the world are men | It is a real law | It is about beavers
test: Erin from Cork: «yer man the Stranger was a right eejit to run off.» (eejit = idiot) Was he? | No: he was brave and polite and tried his best to help | Yes, he never meant to help at all and only wanted to get away from the river | Yes, he stole the spear | Yes, he hit the ladies
test: Real life: where did true writing first begin, about 5,000 years ago? | In Mesopotamia, from pictures and counting marks | On Merrow Down in Surrey, with a girl called Taffy | In a cave by the Wagai | In Rome
test: Real life: when did writing reach Britain? | With the Romans, about 2,000 years ago | In the Stone Age, with Taffy and her Daddy | 200 years ago | It never did
test: Ben from Sunderland: «howay, the Wey's always been this size.» (howay = come on) What does the poem say? | It was more than six times bigger then | It was exactly the same size as it is today | It was a small brook | It was dry
test: Looking back: in part 1, what did Taffy wish they could do to get the spear? | Write, so they could send a message home | Run very fast | Swim home | Shout loud enough for Mummy to hear them back at the Cave
test: Looking back: in part 1, what did Taffy pull from the Stranger-man? | The shark's tooth from his necklace | His hair | His spear | His birch-bark, which he had kept for his own little girl-daughter
test: Looking back: the Tribe was wrong about the picture. Which earlier character was wrong about a nose that would not shrink? | The Elephant's Child | Painted Jaguar | The Camel | The Kangaroo
test: Looking back: like Taffy, Old Man Kangaroo got more than he asked for. What did he ask? | To be made different and popular by five o'clock | To be made strong | To have a pouch | To be left alone to dance on his rock in the middle of Australia
test: Josh: «I'm dead, the ladies said "Idiot!" ever so politely.» (I'm dead = that's so funny) Why is it a joke? | Calling someone «Idiot!» is not polite at all | They really were polite | «Idiot» means husband | They were talking to the beavers
test: ★★★ «Then the Head Chief laughed; then the Stranger-man; then Tegumai; then all the Tribe.» Who laughed third? | Tegumai | The Stranger-man | All the Tribe | The Head Chief
test: ★★★ The Head Chief says «some day men will call it writing. At present it is only pictures.» What is the difference he means? | Writing fixes exact words, pictures do not | Writing is drawn with a shark's tooth, pictures with a stick | Pictures are bigger | Writing has colours
test: ★★★ The poem says Taffy lived on Merrow Down «long and long before» the ancient Britons traded there. If the Britons traded about 2,500 years ago, when did Taffy live? | Even earlier than that | At just the same time as the ancient Britons traded there | After the Romans | Last year
exam: Identify the explanation furnished by Taffy in respect of the «bad people» depicted upon the birch-bark. | That they were beavers, drawn to indicate the way through the swamp | That they were enemies of the Tribe | That they were Neolithic ladies | That they were the soldiers of the Head Chief, advancing in Platoons and Detachments
exam: Ascertain the designation by which, according to the Head Chief, the invention would hereafter be known. | Writing | Painting | Engraving | Picture-telling
exam: Select the response most consistent with the established facts concerning the fate of the Stranger-man. | He was adopted into the Tribe, having made no fuss about the mud | He was banished from the banks of the Wagai for ever after | He was beheaded | He returned to his own tribe in anger
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the arrival of writing in Britain? | It arrived with the Romans, approximately two thousand years ago | It was invented in Surrey during the Stone Age | It arrived in the present century, by means of the telephone | It has never arrived
